course: arts
lesson: 28
title: The school play: auditions, lines, stage fright
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A school play starts with auditions, where everyone tries out and the director picks who fits each part, and every part, even "Tree Number 3", is needed to tell the story.
explain: Then come weeks of rehearsals: learning lines out loud, learning where to stand, turning a little towards the audience so they can see your face, and practising the bows at the end.
explain: On the big night your heart races and your tummy flips; that is stage fright, your body's alarm going off, and even famous actors feel it every time.
explain: Something always goes a bit wrong live, a ripped costume or a lost line, and the trick is to stay calm and carry on, because the audience at a school play is rooting for you.
explain: People say it their own way: "gutted" means very disappointed, "hurl" means be sick, "legend" means a hero, and "smashing" is an old British word for great.
fact: In an audition people try out, and the director chooses who fits each part; small parts matter, and many famous actors started with tiny roles.
fact: Actors learn lines by saying them aloud again and again, often while moving, and by learning the cue, the line just before theirs.
fact: Stage fright is very common, even for famous actors; slow deep breaths and good practice help.
fact: Cheating out means standing a little open to the audience so they can see your face, even while talking to another actor.
fact: Live shows often have small disasters, so teams keep safety pins, tape and spare costumes ready.
fact: Corpsing is theatre slang for laughing on stage when you should not; it happens to everyone, and practising the funny bit until it stops being funny helps.
fact: If you lose your place, staying calm and carrying on usually works, and school audiences are friendly.
fact: Many parents work shifts and sometimes miss shows; it does not mean they do not care, and filming the show can help.
fact: After a show the director gives praise and notes, small changes to make it better next time; good work can still improve.
fact: The curtain call is the bows at the end, and it is rehearsed, often with the lead bowing last.
exercise: Hugo wanted the lead and got "Tree Number 3". What is the wisest move? | Play the tree brilliantly | Refuse to be in the play at all until the teacher gives him a better part | Act the tree badly on purpose so that the teacher finally notices him | Tell everyone in the class that small parts are pointless and boring
exercise: What is an audition? | A try-out for a part in a play | The bows at the end of a show | A costume for the main actor | The script of the play
exercise: Amira's gran makes her say her lines while walking round the kitchen. Why does it work? | Saying lines aloud while moving helps | Kitchens are warm, and warm rooms make people much cleverer at school | Her gran was secretly saying the lines for her, so she only had to listen | Kitchens help memory
exercise: What is a cue? | The line or action just before yours | A long stick used in snooker only | The last bow of the show | The ticket for the play
exercise: Noah's knees shake on opening night. What is happening? | Common stage fright | He is ill and must be sent home at once before the show starts | It means he is bad at acting and should not have been given a part | It only ever happens to beginners and stops after the first play
exercise: What helps with stage fright? | Slow deep breaths and good practice | Running round the stage before the show | Eating lots of sweets backstage | Pretending the audience is not there by closing your eyes
exercise: The teacher tells Ella to "cheat out". What does she mean? | Turn a bit towards the audience | Copy someone else's lines from the script when she forgets her own | Leave the stage a little early before the end of her scene | Whisper her lines
exercise: Jamal's costume rips. What saves the day? | Safety pins | Going on stage with no costume at all and hoping nobody notices | Stopping the whole play until a brand new costume can be made | Hiding backstage until the play is over so nobody sees the rip
exercise: Lily keeps giggling at Harry's line. What is that called? | Corpsing | Cheating out | Projecting | Prompting
exercise: How can Lily stop corpsing? | Practise the funny bit till it is not funny | Never look at the script again so she cannot remember what is funny | Laugh even louder in the real show so it looks like part of the play | Skip the funny bit
exercise: Sean forgets his place and says "loads happened". The audience laughs. What is the lesson? | Stay calm and carry on | The whole show is ruined for ever and everybody will remember it | He should have run off the stage and let someone else finish | Narrators should never speak at all, they should only hold signs
exercise: Mei-Lin's parents cannot come because of work. Which is true? | It does not mean they do not care | It means they do not love her as much as other parents love their children | Parents always manage to come to every show if they really care | Work never matters
exercise: The director says "smashing!" and then gives notes. Can both be true? | Yes, good work can still get better | No, notes mean the show was bad | No, praise means nothing needs fixing | Yes, but only for the lead actor
exercise: What are notes from a director? | Small changes to improve the next show | Letters to parents about bad behaviour | Music for the orchestra | Tickets for the next show
exercise: Kai bows too early, alone. What helps avoid that? | Rehearsing the curtain call too | Bowing first every time | Never bowing at all | Bowing whenever you feel like it
exercise: Do famous actors get stage fright? | Yes, many feel it every time | No, only school kids get it | No, famous actors are trained out of it | Only actors who forget their lines
test: Your mate goes, "Stage fright means you're rubbish at acting, simple." What is the right answer? | Many great performers feel it | He is right, good actors never feel nervous before they go on stage | Only very shy people ever feel it, confident people never do | It lasts for ever
test: What would happen if every actor turned their back to the audience? | Nobody could see their faces or hear them well | The play would look more real | The audience would see more | The lights would work better
test: A Geordie kid says his part is "canny small, like" ("canny" means quite). What is the best reply? | Every part is needed | Small parts are only given to people who cannot act very well | He should quit the play and sit in the audience to watch instead | Small parts do not need any rehearsing, so he can skip practice
test: Your cousin says actors just "have perfect memories, innit". Which is true? | They repeat lines until they stick | They are born able to remember everything they ever read or hear | They secretly read their lines from cards hidden around the stage | They make lines up
test: In a cartoon, a kid's costume rips and the whole show stops for an hour. What usually happens in real life? | The team fixes it fast with pins or tape | The show is cancelled | The audience goes home | The actor has to perform in pyjamas
test: Your friend from Texas says she's "fixin' to hurl" ("fixin' to" means about to) before going on. What could help? | Slow, deep breaths | Running away from the school before anyone can find her | Eating a big meal just before she goes on stage to calm her tummy | Telling herself over and over again that she will mess up
test: The director gives Oscar notes after praising the show. Oscar says, "Make your mind up, miss." What is the misunderstanding? | Praise and notes can both be true | Notes mean that the praise before them was just a polite lie | Praise means there is nothing at all left to fix for tomorrow | Notes mean it was bad
test: Two kids keep giggling in rehearsal and the teacher says "you're corpsing". What does she mean? | They are laughing on stage | They are pretending to be dead bodies, which is not in the script | They are standing in the wrong place, too far from the audience | They forgot their lines
test: Which is true about the bows at the end of a play? | They are rehearsed | They happen at random whenever each actor feels like bowing | Only the lead actor is allowed to bow, the rest stand still | Bows are not part of the show, so nobody ever practises them
test: Mei-Lin is gutted her parents cannot come. What is a kind fix? | Film the show for them or tell them all about it | Tell her they do not care | Cancel the play for everyone | Make her do the play another day alone
test: Why should you learn your cue as well as your lines? | So you know exactly when to speak | So you can say other people's lines | So you can skip rehearsals | So you can leave the stage early
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a performer is instructed to "cheat out" upon the stage. | So that the audience may see the face and hear the voice | So that the performer may read lines concealed in the wings of the stage | So that the performer may exit from the stage with greater speed when required | To face the stage crew
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the condition designated "stage fright"? | A common nervous response experienced even by accomplished performers | A reliable indication of inadequate ability | An ailment restricted to inexperienced children | A condition requiring the cancellation of the performance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term "corpsing". | Involuntary laughter on stage | It denotes the portrayal of a deceased character by a member of the cast | It denotes the forgetting of a line during a performance before an audience | The final bow of the cast
exam: Ascertain the response most appropriate where a performer loses their place during a school performance. | The maintenance of composure and continuation of the performance | The abandonment of the stage for the remainder of the evening | The demand that the performance recommence from its beginning | The reproach of the audience for any laughter
